>On Fri, 25 Mar 1994, Tim Starr wrote:
>
>> You're buying into what Kelley calls the "diaphanous" model of con-
>> sciousness, that in order for direct awareness to be possible, it would
>> have to be unmediated by any faculty of awareness.  That's not what I'm
>> talking about.
>
>Once again you have displayed your talent for neatly labeling the
>opponent's argument. Could you perhaps share with us the things you find
>*problematic* about the diaphanous model of consciousness, whatever that
>might be? 

Sure: it has it that if the nature of consciousness affects the awareness of
the objects of knowledge in any way, then it can't be authentic knowledge.
And the only way for this to be avoided is for consciousness not to have any
nature or identity, which means it wouldn't exist at all.  Reductio ad absur-
dum: the diaphanous view of consciousness entails the impossibility of con-
sciousness.

>I still have no idea what magical property you are referring to when you 
>say "direct awareness". Perhaps you could provide a genus and a 
>differentia, and then explain why it makes a difference whether awareness 
>is "direct" or not.

I could, or I could just be lazy and tell you to read Kelley's book on the
subject.  Take advantage of the division of labor!

>What exactly do you find problematic
>about Hume's theory of causality, and how do those problems apply to this
>discussion? 

The problem with Hume's theory of causality is precisely the one he made
such a big deal out of: if it is accepted, then induction is impossible.
I've no idea how this applies other than the fact that you brought it up
and seemed to find it relevant.  The point is that 1) that's not the only
theory of causality, and 2)the comparative advantage of the other theory of
causality is that it doesn't suffer from this problem.  If it is accepted,
then induction is possible.

Now I'm sure I'm going to get lots of "What is this theory?" replies, and
complaints about how I'm not explaining my position very well.  True, but
my experience has been that when I do explain myself, then my position gets
attacked for being "metaphysical," not in accordance with modern science
(an instance of the common practice fallacy), beating around the bush and/
or belaboring the obvious.  So, to all those who would come up with any
one of those: do your own homework, or pay me to do it for you.  I've got
lots of other things to do besides go to lots of trouble for nothing.

>If I took the statement on faith, I would not be interested in listening
>to your attempts at refutation, because they could make no difference to
>my faith-based belief.

This doesn't follow.  I've experienced lots of people who are interested in
paying attention to my refutations of their faiths, in spite of the fact
that they can make no difference to their beliefs.

> But not only am I profoundly interested in what
>your refutations might be, I am eager to hear them, and disappointed that
>you refrain from providing them. 

It's because I got burned on another list recently.  Not your fault, or
the fault of anyone on this list, but I went to the trouble to compose a
200-line point-by-point counter-argument to a poster on the children's
rights list, only to get the lame response of: "Well, if Tim thinks that,
then he must be out of touch with reality."  I'm going to take a break from
long expositions, so read Kelley instead.

>To say that I have no reason to believe it is also incorrect. My claim is
>that I cannot logically deduce it from a principle or set of principles
>that would not themselves be subject to your demand for justification and
>proof. That is not equivalent to a claim that I have no reason to believe
>it. 

There's some other reason to believe it that you're hiding?

>You have declined my invitation to refute my view,

That's partly because I'm not as interested in refuting it as understanding
it.  I guess I'll have to try to read Bartley some more, but I just don't
have a good enough grasp on PCR yet to try to refute it.

>How about I refute objectivism, eh? If you 
>care to explain it, that is, in the same manner that I have attempted to 
>explain pancritical rationalism.

Maybe some other time.

>1) The theory requires the existence of a material world
>2) The theory is correct
>3) therefore, a material world exists.
>
>That's a simple syllogism, Tim. I don't see anything circular in it at all.
>
>Or are you going to try to claim that we cannot find the theory correct
>without presuming a material world?

Precisely.

> Bishop Berkeley can explain the fallacy of that to you.

I know Berkeley quite well, thank you, having written a critique of his
position for an epistemology class in college.  Citing him as your authority
in refutation of my position is inconsistent both with your espoused realism
and anti-justificationism, since he was a justificationist idealist.

Nor is Berkeley's position consistent with my position on axioms and what may
be derived from them.  Berkeley's whole system is founded upon the alleged
axiom that to be is to be perceived (esse is percipi), which isn't an axiom.
Test it: can it be denied without presupposing it?  "To be is not to be
perceived."  In what way does this statement presuppose that esse is percipi?
None.  Thus, his entire positionc collapses like a house of cards, once his
foundational premise is removed.

Henry Lous Mencken once said that all philosophers occupy themselves with
proving every other philosopher wrong - and that they were all right.  In
this spirit, Berkeley was right only insofar as he proved representationalism
wrong, not insofar as he argued for idealism.

>...Darwin's theory 
>in and of itself does not presume materialism for anyone but an atheist. 

Incorrect.  Darwin's theory doesn't presume _reductive_ materialism, or monist
materialism, the view that everything is matter, but it does presume that at
least some material world exists.

