First of all, there's no reason why individual victims would have to go
it alone.  They could join together in a class-action suit.  Secondly, it's
not so "vast an impossible" to prove joint-commission of torts, once you've
established the tort and its victims.  Then the remedy can be restitution
from any and all of the tortfeasors (the victims could sell collection rights
to the highest-bidding collection agency) and injunctive relief against any
and all future tortfeasors.  What's so tough about any of that?  It ain't
perfect, but what better way is there?

>But, on the other hand, I don't really see fairly modest regulation
>being very harmful to the development of those technological solutions
>(as long as it stays fairly modest).

Regulation never "stays fairly modest."  Regulations are issued by agencies on
their own authority, with only the vaguest guidance from Congress, enforced by
law-enforcement agents that don't even have the review commissions many cities
have for their police, adjudicated by administrative law judges without a jury
of one's peers, and punishable by civil asset forfeiture of all your transfer-
able tangible assets - every single part of which violates almost every single
procedure and precept of the Constitution, Bill of Rights, and Anglo-American
common law.  Not only are such conditions immodest to begin with, but their
incentives are towards ever-increasing immodesty, with the only check on their
rapacity being the people's willingness to kill the agents in self-defense and
acquit each other for it when brought to trial for conspiracy to pursue one's
own happiness in defiance of the State.

